WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.320
- Vote no on the recall,
ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.248
Our Governor, Gavin Newsom.

00:00:05.248 --> 00:00:06.450
(crowd cheering)

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.620
- [Narrator] California
Governor Gavin Newsom is at risk

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.550
of being voted out of office on Tuesday

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:12.459
in a rare special election.

00:00:12.459 --> 00:00:13.308
- [Protestor] Recall.

00:00:13.308 --> 00:00:14.174
- [Crowd] Newsom.

00:00:14.174 --> 00:00:15.023
- Recall.

00:00:15.023 --> 00:00:16.290
- [Crowd] Newsom.

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:18.240
- [Narrator] So how did
a Democratic politician

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.070
who sailed to victory in a
Blue state end up becoming

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.550
only the second governor
in California history

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:24.780
to face a recall?

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.740
Newsom's handling of the
COVID-19 pandemic in California

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.800
has become the most
prominent issue in the race.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.320
- We have autonomy over our own body

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.797
to give ourself what we feel
is the proper medical treatment

00:00:34.797 --> 00:00:36.598
for our condition.

00:00:36.598 --> 00:00:37.530
And if we don't want the vaccine,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:38.970
we shouldn't have to take it.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.850
- I think he's done a good
job with COVID in response.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:43.810
I'd like to see him continue his term.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:45.198
- Depending on who you're asking,

00:00:45.198 --> 00:00:48.530
Gavin Newsom either crippled the economy

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.820
and closed schools,
and left kids to suffer

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:53.130
in virtual learning,

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.610
or he took the tough
but necessary measures

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.150
to keep Californians safe.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.430
And so that's kind of really
a test in this election.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.050
Who are you going to believe.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.920
- [Narrator] Recall efforts
are not uncommon in California.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.100
In fact, every governor since 1960

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.040
has faced a petition for removal.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.070
And for Newsom, this
is one of six attempts

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:14.620
by Republican activists
since he was elected in 2018.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:15.810
But it's been very rare

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.270
for those recall attempts

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:19.010
to actually reach the ballot.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:20.700
In order for a recall to be triggered,

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:21.960
the petition has to be signed

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.500
by enough verified signatures

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.700
to equal 12% of the votes cast

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.800
in the last gubernatorial election.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.340
In the state's only other
gubernatorial recall,

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.910
Republican Arnold Schwarzenegger

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:35.830
replaced Democrat Gray
Davis as governor in 2003.

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:39.270
So what has contributed to
this latest recall effort?

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.210
The original petition was written shortly

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:42.520
before the pandemic hit,

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.530
and cites common complaints
that conservatives

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:46.010
have about California:

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.080
High taxes, layers of regulation

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.500
and policies favorable to immigrants.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.840
But Newsom's troubles grew

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.160
as the pandemic took hold

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.130
and many Californians became frustrated

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:56.960
by restrictions and mandates,

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.180
including policies that closed businesses,

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.380
schools and houses of worship.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:04.710
Then last November, the
recall effort gained steam.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:08.050
On the 13th, as COVID-19
cases across the state began

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:10.322
to rise, state officials asked residents

00:02:10.322 --> 00:02:13.640
to avoid gathering with people
outside their households.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.760
Four days later, news photos surfaced,

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.590
showing the governor dining maskless

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.920
with lobbyist friends
at The French Laundry,

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.300
an exclusive Napa Valley restaurant.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.140
By chance, earlier that day,

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.600
a Sacrament County judge
gave recall petitioners

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.050
an extra four months to gather signatures,

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.300
citing closures due to the pandemic.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.700
- It really encapsulated
the anger that people had

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.780
over what they felt was his hypocrisy,

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.030
and his privilege,

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:40.700
this idea of rules for thee and not for me

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:43.440
was just this overarching theme

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.250
that really drove a lot of people's anger,

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.290
and caused them to sign this petition.

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:48.560
- [Narrator] Newsom has said that dinner

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:50.120
was an error in judgment.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.160
Within weeks, the total
number of signatures

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.010
on the petition to recall Newsom grew

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:57.920
from about 56,000 to nearly 500,000.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:02:59.880
By April, the petition had collected

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.200
more than 1.7 million signatures

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.900
to qualify for the ballot.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.450
- Before the recall officially qualified,

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:09.420
the campaign's strategy
was really avoidance

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.420
and really not talking about the recall.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.680
- [Narrator] This spring,
as COVID-19 cases started

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:16.210
to level out, Newsom projected optimism.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.101
- California has the lowest case rate

00:03:18.101 --> 00:03:20.210
in the United States of America,

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:22.380
which is significant when
you consider the size

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:26.010
and scale of our state,
the density in this state.

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.070
- He really tried to telegraph

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:29.450
that the state was on the mend

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:31.120
and good times were coming.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.240
That kind of all changed

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.349
when Larry Elder joined
the race this summer.

00:03:34.349 --> 00:03:36.320
- There are several big issues.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.450
The biggest one is the
rise of homelessness,

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:42.200
the rise in crime, the
outrageous cost of living.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:44.350
- [Narrator] A conservative
radio host from Los Angeles,

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.010
Elder quickly became the
top polling Republican

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.670
ahead of 45 other
candidates on the ballot.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:52.360
His platform includes
increasing home construction

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:54.100
to reduce the state's high prices,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.510
school vouchers and tougher
anti-crime policies.

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.670
Elder has also criticized
Newsom's COVID-19 restrictions

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:04.050
and has pledged to end
vaccine and mask mandates.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:05.040
- Right now, this government

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.370
has required every state worker

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:08.800
who's not been vaccinated
to be tested once a week

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:10.580
and to wear a face mask at work.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.840
When I become governor, one of
the first things I'm gonna do

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.580
is to repeal that mandate.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.650
- [Narrator] Even though
polls project Newsom winning,

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:18.450
the race has turned out to be much closer

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.110
than experts initially expected,

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:21.920
prompting some of the
most prominent people

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:24.200
in the Democratic party
to campaign for him.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:29.130
- Protect California by voting
no on the Republican recall.

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:31.610
- [Narrator] The special
election is local to Californians

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:33.410
but some experts argue the outcome

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.980
of the vote has broader significance.

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.120
- There's plenty of
people who are disgruntled

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.620
about Newsom's COVID shutdowns,

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.260
about his mask mandates,
about his vaccine mandates,

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:44.770
and this is really a test

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:48.680
as to whether people approve
of those policies or not.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.980
In terms of the national context,

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:55.980
this is really significant
in terms of a peek into 2022

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:59.320
in terms of engaging voters,

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:03.270
in terms of whether or not COVID response

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:06.063
is a winning message for Democrats.

